Document 2: Model Architecture Specification
Document date: December 2, 2025. This is the earliest formal specification of the multi-model architecture.
Code

Core Architecture: Fully Connected Agent Mesh

The system uses a fully connected mesh topology where every agent
node communicates bidirectionally with every other node. No single
point of failure, no hierarchical bottleneck.

Agent Nodes:
Node  | Parameters | Function
3BM   | 3B         | Memory
3BR   | 3B         | Reason
3BC   | 3B         | Code
8BM   | 8B         | Memory
8BR   | 8B         | Reason
8BC   | 8B         | Code
14BM  | 14B        | Memory
14BR  | 14B        | Reason
14BC  | 14B        | Code
9BF   | 9B         | Face (orchestration)

Design Principle:
Each functional role (Memory, Reason, Code) is instantiated at
multiple parameter scales (3B, 8B, 14B), allowing the system to
route tasks to the appropriate depth of processing. The 9BF node
serves as the orchestration layer.

What This Was
This December 2025 specification documents the Mixture of Experts architecture at the heart of the original Irwin system. Rather than using a single large model, the system distributed cognitive work across specialized nodes — dedicated memory agents, reasoning agents, and code agents — at three different parameter scales. The 9BF orchestration node managed routing decisions.
This is the earliest formal documentation of the project. Its December 2025 date establishes the project timeline.
Why the Architecture Evolved Past This
The MoE mesh architecture was technically sound and proved the distributed cognition concept. The evolution wasn't away from distribution — it was toward a cleaner implementation of it.
The explicit node structure (3BM, 3BR, 3BC, etc.) was a workaround for the absence of a natural language routing layer. Each node had to be explicitly defined because there was no intelligent layer to decide dynamically which model to invoke for which task.
The Router V3 specification (documented separately) was the bridge — a formal routing system that made escalation and de-escalation intelligent rather than hardcoded. The current AxiomOS architecture takes this further: the natural language shell handles intent classification, the constitutional layer evaluates requests, and the model routing happens dynamically based on task complexity rather than explicit node assignment.
What Replaced It
The Router V3 escalation model replaces the fixed node topology. A micro model handles intent classification and simple tasks. Escalation to larger models happens only when complexity requires it. De-escalation is mandatory after task completion to avoid model inertia. The constitutional layer governs all routing decisions.
The spirit of the MoE architecture — right model for the right task, no single point of failure — survives in the current design. The explicit node naming convention does not.